1. Q1: Name the four stages a C source file passes through on its way to an executable, and what each stage takes as input and produces 
as output.

The 4 stages that a C source file passes through are Preprocessing, Compilation, Assembly, and Linking. Preprocessing takes raw .c source 
and produces pure C source with #include/#define directives being expanded. Compilation takes that pure C source and produces assembly code.
Assembly takes that code and creates an object file (.o). Finally, linking takes the object files plus any needed libraries and 
produces a single executable line across file boundaries.


2. Q2: What does -Wall add to a gcc invocation? What does -Wextra add beyond -Wall? What does -Werror change about how warnings are treated?
-Wall adds a broad set of common warnings to the code that looks for things like unused variables, suspicious comparisons, 
format-string mismatches, and other common warnings. -Wextra adds additional warnings that -Wall doesn’t over, warnings like unused 
function parameters, stricter sign-comparison checks, and more. -Werror makes it so that if the file has an error, it does not compile 
until all errors have been resolved.

3. Q3: What is the difference between compiling a file with gcc -c versus without -c?
Compiling a file with gcc -c goes through the preprocessing, compilation, and assembly stages and produces a relocatable object file but 
doesn’t go through the linking stage. Compiling a file without the -c goes through all of the stages including linking and produces a 
complete executable file.

4. Q4: In a Makefile rule, what are the target, the prerequisites, and the recipe?
In a make file the target is file the rule produces. The prerequisites are the files that the target depends on. The recipe is the shell 
command(s) on the indented line.

5. Q5: Why does make usually avoid rebuilding a target that is already up to date, and how does it decide?
Make avoids rebuilding a target because if a target already exists and is newer than every one of the prerequisites, make considers it 
up to date and skips the recipe entirely. Make will rebuild a target if it doesn’t exist yet, or if any direct prerequisite is newer 
than the target.

6. Q6: What is the difference between a local git commit and pushing that commit — why can a commit exist and still not count as submitted work for this course?
The local git commit only changes the history of your local git repository and it doesn’t exist until you push it. Pushing the commit 
sends the committed changes to the remote repository.
